    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n40062" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">quattŭor</span></b> , less correctly <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">quātŭor</span></b>  (also
		written <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">quattor</span></b> ), num. adj.
		kindr. with Sanscr. catur; Gr. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τέτταρες</span> from <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τέτϝαρες</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>four</i>: ter quattuor corpora, Enn. ap. <i><span class="en">Cic. Div.
				1, 48, 108</span></i> (Ann. v. 96 Vahl.); <i><span class="en">Plaut.
				Most. 3, 1, 102</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Cic.
				Univ. 5</span></i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ex centum quattuor centuriis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					Rep. 2, 22, 39</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">tria aut quattuor paria amicorum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					Lael. 4, 15</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Hor. S. 1, 3, 8</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Verg. E. 5,
				65</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Juv. 5, 47</span></i>.</div>
